WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.040
Hi!

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:04.680
This morning, I caught Ms. Coffee Bean watching…

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:11.000
the PDF of this paper entitled “Using AntiPatterns
to avoid MLOps Mistakes”.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.710
Ms. Coffee Bean, what were you thinking?

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:19.270
I guess you read it because you have little
to no idea about machine learning in the real

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:20.270
world.

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:25.160
Haha, no wonder since you are in academia
where all the work you do

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:26.830
ends in a paper.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:32.160
But you know, there is life to machine learning
methods even after they have been presented

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.350
in a paper;
life you would like to know more about.

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:40.900
Okay, Ms. Coffee Bean, since I do not know
what else to do with you, what about this:

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:46.850
I found the data leakage antipattern the most
interesting one, but the writeup was quite

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:50.320
dense (since the paper is made for experts).

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.930
Let’s make it accessible to anyone by explaining
this together!

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:56.510
Alright, Ms. Coffee Bean!

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:01.989
This paper is sharing lessons about MLOps,
which is all about putting machine learning

00:01:01.989 --> 00:01:07.390
models into production,
but also about maintaining the whole operation.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:13.320
However, the lessons of this paper are not
about what to do, and good strategies and

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:20.450
patterns, but more about what not to do, so
about the antipatterns (or antipatterns).

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:25.760
Clearly, the authors summarize their experience
here, which means that the lessons are more

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:28.560
tailored for the financial sector.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.729
But don’t worry though, they’re applicability
is much wider than this.

00:01:32.729 --> 00:01:38.810
The paper guides us through antipatterns ranging
from technical errors, to cases of missing

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:43.350
knowledge about the context in which machine
learning is deployed.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:51.110
Data leakage is one of 9 antipatterns in this
paper… yeah each with catchy names.

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:57.030
But data leakage is the most interesting one
to me because these days, I have been interested

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:01.909
in the bigger question of model generalization.

00:02:01.909 --> 00:02:08.110
Since the train-dev-test split is one very
weak test for model generalization,

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:13.569
data leakage is the one responsible for not
letting us perform even this simple baseline

00:02:13.569 --> 00:02:15.400
for generalization.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:21.200
Data leakage is a violation of one of the
first axioms that we are taught in every machine

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:25.189
learning course:
Do not let samples from the test set leak

00:02:25.189 --> 00:02:31.230
into your training set and vice-versa, otherwise
you will overestimate your performance since

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:35.049
your model is already familiar with the data
you are testing on.

00:02:35.049 --> 00:02:39.430
So how can this be an antipattern if everybody
knows about it?

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:41.840
And, it is quite easy to implement, right?

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:48.849
Well, no, it is not that easy to ensure total
separation between train and test data.

00:02:48.849 --> 00:02:53.879
The authors report four ways of accidentally
enabling data leakage.

00:02:53.879 --> 00:02:56.379
Each one is sneakier than the other.

00:02:56.379 --> 00:02:57.829
Okay, great.

00:02:57.829 --> 00:03:00.709
We are ready to see the data leakage antipatterns.

00:03:00.709 --> 00:03:01.810
Shoot!

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:07.129
The Peek-a-Boo Antipattern is specific to
time-series data.

00:03:07.129 --> 00:03:10.879
It is described in this little cryptic paragraph.

00:03:10.879 --> 00:03:13.840
Now here is what Ms. Coffee Bean understood
from it:

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.819
Let’s suppose we have a time series, and
for splitting the data between train and test

00:03:18.819 --> 00:03:25.040
set, we could say we take all the data up
to a certain time point t to be training data

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:30.010
and all the data after timepoint t to be test
data.

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:36.239
The splitting pattern could be also more complicated,
but the principle of the peek-a-boo antipattern

00:03:36.239 --> 00:03:37.879
works the same.

00:03:37.879 --> 00:03:44.559
The problem arises when time-series datasets
have at least two dates: the date of the actual

00:03:44.559 --> 00:03:50.180
time stamp of the data point, but also for
example the date when the data becomes available

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:57.329
like in job salary data, which becomes available
in March but speaks about February, so about

00:03:57.329 --> 00:03:58.989
the last month.

00:03:58.989 --> 00:04:05.260
This time lag and the exact timing of things
might be unknown to the machine learning engineer

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:11.059
and messing around timestamps can really mess
up the train-test separation by accidentally

00:04:11.059 --> 00:04:15.080
including data from the train set, from the
past,

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:17.590
into the test set, from the future.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:22.620
Combine this with a feature that is highly
predictive of another feature and we will

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:27.950
have completely overestimated
our abilities of predicting the future through

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:28.950
this lag.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:33.770
So, what is the real lesson here apart from
train-test separation?

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:39.330
Know the data you are trying to model, know
what every tiny feature means!

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:43.900
The next data leakage antipattern called “temporal
leakage”

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:46.550
is also related to time-series data.

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:51.030
With an ideal test set, we would estimate
the model’s true performance when deployed

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:52.860
into the real world.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:57.320
Ideally the test set would be independent
from the training set to estimate how well

00:04:57.320 --> 00:05:00.500
the model performs in unexpected situations.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:05.350
But this paper warns us that if we take a
time-series data and sample train and test

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:11.230
sets from it in a non-sequential manner, then
the two are not independent anymore because

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:14.950
we have technically seen the future during
training already.

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:22.150
So, we have turned this into an easier interpolation
problem what was previously an extrapolation

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:25.490
problem of predicting the future, which is
harder.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:27.590
The next type of leakage is “oversampling”.

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:33.840
It talks specifically about oversampling techniques
that are usually applied if one class is very

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:40.390
underrepresented in the data; Strategies like
SMOTE – or similar methods – basically

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:46.500
interpolate between the few existing points
to create new points.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:52.220
So in a sense, the data has been smeared out
to have a larger population in the feature

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:53.220
space.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:58.490
But you see the problem: The new interpolated
points would be informed about all points,

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:00.650
from train and test alike.

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:06.770
Therefore, if we split only after the oversampling
strategy, we would have contaminated the test

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:11.500
set with train set information and also the
other way around, of course.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:17.550
So from antipatterns 2 and 3 we can remember:
the order of data pre-processing steps and

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:22.100
splitting into train and test splits, matters!

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:27.050
The last mentioned antipattern responsible
for data leakage is called “metrics from

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:28.050
beyond”.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:32.700
To Ms. Coffee Bean, this is very similar to
the oversampling strategy.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:38.670
This talks about other operations, that if
performed before train-test splitting, could

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:42.050
smear out information from one set to the
other.

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:47.370
One example could be data normalization by
subtracting the mean and dividing by the variance

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:48.740
for example.

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:54.440
If we do this for train and test together,
the normalization operation informs the future

00:06:54.440 --> 00:07:01.280
train set of the mean and variance of the
whole set, including the test set; and vice-versa,

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:02.400
of course.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:06.670
Which data leakage antipattern is the one
that you hate the most?

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:12.110
And if you are honest: which one of these
traps have you fallen into at least once so

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:15.280
that you had learned the lesson forever?

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:19.160
What are other data leakage examples that
you would like to share with us?

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:23.860
If you are interested in what you have seen
so far, read the whole paper to find out about

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:26.520
all other anti... antipatterns.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:32.389
Until Ms. Coffee Bean will see you again,
repeat after her: “always separate your

00:07:32.389 --> 00:07:34.990
train and test data like your life depends
on it!”

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:35.000
Okay bye!

